From: j7
Subject: j7: mux the gauge's own I2C pins and ask that bus first

Samsung's on7xelte device tree answers where the fuel gauge is: `s2mu005-fuelgauge@34` with
`reg = <0x3B>` on `i2c@13830000`, the controller the SoC calls I2C_4 (I2C_FUELGAUGE), whose
pins are gpc4-0 and gpc4-1 at function 2.  It is not on hsi2c0: that is the PMIC bus, and it
carries the charger, not the gauge.  The register map the driver already uses - RSOC 0x08,
RVBAT 0x04, RCUR_CC 0x06, RBATCAP 0x0e - is exactly the s2mu005 header's.

The scan reached 0x13830000 and logged `stat=0xf0 con=0xe3`: the controller took every value
written and the transfer never left the block.  Its PCLK gate is already open (CMU_PERI +0x0810
bit 21), so what is missing is the pinmux.  Pin-controller 6 (TOP) is at 0x139b0000, gpc4 is
its bank at 0x060, and bank_type_4 puts CON at +0x00 and PUD at +0x08 there; set the two
nibbles to function 2 and both pull-ups on, logging the words before and after.

Ask 0x13830000 first, then hsi2c0 and the rest as before, so the bus the board file names is
the one that gets the first ACK.

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

--- a/bsd/dev/a5xelte_battery.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/a5xelte_battery.c
@@ -392,6 +392,37 @@
 	printf("A5XELTE FG: CMU_PERI gates 0x%08x -> 0x%08x -> 0x%08x\n", before, want, after);
 }
 
+/* i2c@13830000 is Samsung's I2C_4_FUELGAUGE controller, and its pins are gpc4-0 (SDA) and
+ * gpc4-1 (SCL), function 2 with the internal pull-ups on.  They live in pin-controller 6
+ * (TOP, 0x139b0000); gpc4 is the bank at 0x060, and bank_type_4 puts CON at +0x00 and PUD at
+ * +0x08 in that bank.  The bootloader muxes only the PMIC bus (hsi2c0), so without this the
+ * controller is clocked, its registers take the values written, and the transfer never leaves
+ * the block: exactly the stat=0xf0 the scan logged. */
+#define FG_PINCTRL_TOP  0x139b0000u
+#define FG_GPC4_CON     0x060u
+#define FG_GPC4_PUD     0x068u
+
+static void
+fg_pins(void)
+{
+	vm_offset_t p = ml_io_map(FG_PINCTRL_TOP, 0x1000);
+
+	if (!p) {
+		printf("A5XELTE FG: no pinctrl map\n");
+		return;
+	}
+	volatile uint32_t *con = (volatile uint32_t *)(p + FG_GPC4_CON);
+	volatile uint32_t *pud = (volatile uint32_t *)(p + FG_GPC4_PUD);
+	uint32_t conBefore = *con, pudBefore = *pud;
+	/* Four bits per pin: gpc4-0 in 3:0, gpc4-1 in 7:4.  PUD: two bits per pin. */
+	*con = (conBefore & ~0xffu) | 0x22u;
+	*pud = (pudBefore & ~0x0fu) | 0x0fu;
+	__asm__ volatile ("dsb sy" ::: "memory");
+	delay(100);
+	printf("A5XELTE FG: gpc4 con %08x -> %08x, pud %08x -> %08x\n",
+	    conBefore, *con, pudBefore, *pud);
+}
+
 int
 a5xelte_fg_init(void)
 {
@@ -402,20 +433,24 @@
 		return -1;
 	}
 	fg_clock_on();
-	/* Samsung's tree puts the S2MU004 gauge on I2C4 at 0x3b; the MAX77854 part that the
-	 * S7 uses keeps its gauge at 0x36 next to the PMIC at 0x66, and both parts appear on
-	 * Exynos 7870 boards.  Ask every address a fuel gauge can have on every controller,
-	 * the bus Samsung documents first, and keep the first pair that ACKs. */
+	fg_pins();
+	/* Samsung's on7xelte device tree puts the S2MU005 gauge on i2c@13830000 at 0x3b, on
+	 * the S3C controller whose pins are gpc4-0/1; its register map is RSOC 0x08, RVBAT
+	 * 0x04, RCUR_CC 0x06 and RBATCAP 0x0e, which is what this driver already reads.
+	 * The MAX77854 part keeps its gauge at 0x36 next to the PMIC at 0x66 and appears on
+	 * other Exynos 7870 boards, so the remaining buses are still asked, but the bus the
+	 * board file names is asked first. */
 	static const struct { uint32_t pa; int hsi2c; } buses[] = {
-		/* hsi2c0, 0x10510000: the PMIC bus, the only controller the bootloader is
-		 * known to have left clocked and pinmuxed, so the gauge is asked here first. */
+		/* i2c_4 / I2C_FUELGAUGE: the gauge's own bus, pins muxed just above. */
+		{ 0x13830000u, 0 },
+		/* hsi2c0, 0x10510000: the PMIC bus, clocked by the bootloader but not muxed
+		 * for a gauge. */
 		{ 0x10510000u, 1 },
 		/* hsi2c1-hsi2c6. */
 		{ 0x138a0000u, 1 }, { 0x138b0000u, 1 }, { 0x138c0000u, 1 },
 		{ 0x138f0000u, 1 }, { 0x13950000u, 1 }, { 0x13960000u, 1 },
-		/* The S3C2440 controllers: i2c0-i2c5, i2c7, i2c8.  They need their pins
-		 * muxed before a transfer moves, which is the stat=0xf0 in the last log. */
-		{ 0x13830000u, 0 }, { 0x13840000u, 0 }, { 0x13850000u, 0 },
+		/* The rest of the S3C2440 controllers: i2c0-i2c3, i2c5, i2c7, i2c8. */
+		{ 0x13840000u, 0 }, { 0x13850000u, 0 },
 		{ 0x13860000u, 0 }, { 0x13870000u, 0 }, { 0x13880000u, 0 },
 		{ 0x13890000u, 0 }, { 0x138d0000u, 0 }, { 0x138e0000u, 0 },
 	};
